 \begin{abstract}
-Classification tasks in healthcare, such as diagnosing a disease or anticipating an adverse outcome, often have to be learned from small datasets, because the disease is rare or because labelling records is expensive, and synthetic data generation can improve the performance of such classifiers by adding artificial records to the training data. The generators in the literature, however, do not address the difficulties of small data: oversampling techniques and deep generative models learn only from the few available records, recombining patterns that already exist in them, and large language models (LLMs), which add domain knowledge from pre-training, return text that often contains fewer records than requested, malformed records or values of the wrong type. In this context, we developed \system, an LLM-based synthetic data generation system for small healthcare datasets whose control layer infers the columns and types of the real data, checks every response of the LLM, asks for the correction of invalid values and requests new records until the configured number is reached, while recording the cost of every request. \system was tested on ten public healthcare datasets with seven LLMs from two providers and compared with three LLM-based and eight established generators. It was the only LLM-based generator that delivered every synthetic dataset at the configured size, at a small additional cost in tokens, and, with the strongest LLM, it gave the largest median F1-score gain among the fourteen generators.
