-To answer these questions, an experimental setup was built with ten public healthcare datasets containing between 116 and 768 records. For each dataset, synthetic records were produced by \system with commercial LLMs from two providers, by three LLM-based baselines (direct prompting, with and without the provider's option to force responses into a fixed format, and the curation approach of CLLM), and by eight established generators from the oversampling, statistical, tree-based and deep families. Each dataset was randomly divided ten times into a training and a test set, and on each division ten classifiers were trained with and without synthetic records and compared on the same test set. The main conclusions are the following. Without the control layer, the LLM never returned the configured number of records, even when its responses were forced into the format of the data, whereas \system completed every dataset at only 1.9\% more cost (RQ1). The synthetic records reproduced the distribution of each real feature with a fidelity of 72 to 77 out of 100, and a membership-inference attack could not reliably identify the real records used, although a classifier could still tell synthetic from real records (RQ2). The effect on classifiers depended on the dataset, the classifier and the LLM: with the strongest LLM, the median F1-score gain was 1.4 percentage points (RQ3). With that LLM, \system gave the largest median gain among fourteen generators; a curation filter improved F1 more often but by less and delivered incomplete datasets, oversampling varied the most, tree-based generators reproduced the distributions more closely at a higher privacy risk, and the parameters of the control layer did not change utility beyond the variation between repeated runs (RQ4).
